Psalm: taint analysis of the CA proxy's PHP
===========================================

CodeQL reads snif's C. The CA proxy's PHP (public_html/, secure_html/,
inc/), which takes requests from anyone, is read by Psalm's taint analysis:
it follows request input to file paths, includes and response headers.
GitHub runs it on every push to master and weekly (.github/workflows/
psalm.yml); a finding outside the baseline is an alert in code scanning,
and gets a verdict before a release, as CodeQL's alerts do: fixed, or read,
found safe and added to the baseline below, with its reason.

    ca-proxy/test/psalm/run.sh                 the analysis (exit 2: findings)
    ca-proxy/test/psalm/run.sh --sarif FILE    the same, findings as SARIF
    ca-proxy/test/psalm/run.sh --canary        checks that Psalm sees the inputs

run.sh fetches Psalm's phar, 6.20.8, pinned by its sha256 (signed by
"Daniil Gentili (Psalm signing key)", 54F5DF0E979A94C40DEDE5E599BF4D9A33D65E1E).
It needs php (cli) and curl. A PHP without mbstring gets byte-wise
stand-ins (mb-shim.php), without which Psalm crashes as it reports a
finding.

The plugin
----------

Psalm treats no $_SERVER value and not the request body as input, so on its
own it finds nothing here. SnifTaintSources.php marks them: $_SERVER and
anything read from it, file_get_contents('php://input'), and the arrays
that hold preg_match() captures of input ($rm, $m, $rspp, since Psalm
loses taint through a capture; a new one goes in its CAPTURES list).

What it still does not see: input carried through $GLOBALS, the body once
assigned to a variable, and symlink(), which is no sink for Psalm. Psalm
does not read a preg_match() check either: a value checked against a
pattern is still input to it, so a path built from a checked name is a
finding, read once and kept in the baseline.

canary/canary.php has one flaw per kind of input the plugin covers; run.sh
--canary fails unless Psalm finds each. CI runs it first, so a plugin that
stops working fails the job instead of passing it clean.

The baseline
------------

psalm-baseline.xml holds the findings read and found safe, by file, kind
and the code at the sink (not by line, so they stay matched as lines move).
Each, as of the version that added it:

public_html/snif-cert.php
  TaintedInclude "$BasePath/inc/snif-cluster.php"
  TaintedFile    "$BasePath/var/snif-certd.ctl"
      $BasePath is DOCUMENT_ROOT/.., which the web server sets; the rest is
      fixed. (The plugin marks all of $_SERVER, DOCUMENT_ROOT too: see
      above.)
  TaintedFile    $authfile, $csr_file, $crt_file, $err_file
  TaintedHeader  "X-SNIF-AuthUrl: $AuthUrl$Domain" (twice)
      $Domain is checked against the hostname pattern
      /^\w([\.\-]?\w)*$/D before any use: no "/", no "..", no newline.
      $AuthBase is $Domain less its first label. $AuthUrl is read from
      etc/, the administrator's. header() refuses a line break anyway.

secure_html/snif-auth.php
  TaintedInclude "$BaseDir/inc/snif-auth-captcha.php" (3), "...ves.php",
                 "$BaseDir/inc/snif-cluster.php"
  TaintedFile    "$BaseDir/var/snif-certd.ctl"
      $BaseDir is DOCUMENT_ROOT/.., as above.
  TaintedFile    "$EtcDir/$Host.domains"
      $Host is checked against the hostname pattern first (400 if not).
  TaintedFile    "$CfgDir/$cn.cfg"
      An allocated name: random hex and a zone from etc/{host}.domains, no
      input in it. What is written in it, the fields, is bounded (README,
      "Allocating a name").
  TaintedFile    file_exists("$EtcDir/$d.keyalgo") ? ... : "$EtcDir/keyalgo"
      $d is a zone from etc/{host}.domains, the administrator's.
  TaintedFile    "$AuthDir/$cn.auth", $pagefile
      $cn, from PATH_INFO, is checked against the hostname pattern before
      either; $AuthBase is $cn less its first label.
  TaintedHeader  "Location: $loc"
      The authorization page's URL from etc/, then this request's own
      https://{Host}{URI}, then &pow=1 or &sitekey=. Only the requester's
      own redirect; the page at snif.host checks the authUrl's origin.
      header() refuses a line break. It is not URL-encoded, since the page
      reads the authUrl parameter as it stands.

To add a finding after reading it, from the repository's root:

    ca-proxy/test/psalm/run.sh --set-baseline=$PWD/ca-proxy/test/psalm/psalm-baseline.xml

and write its reason here. --set-baseline rewrites the whole file from
the findings of that run, so read the diff: it must add only the one read.
